If $k>1$ the image is an **enlargement** — every side is longer than the matching side of the original.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (1.4,0) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (1.4,0) -- (1.4,0.9);
\draw (1.4,0.9) -- (0,0.9);
\draw (0,0.9) -- (0,0);
\node[above] at (0.7,0.9) {Original};
\draw (2.6,0) -- (5.4,0) node[midway,below] {$ka$};
\draw (5.4,0) -- (5.4,1.8);
\draw (5.4,1.8) -- (2.6,1.8);
\draw (2.6,1.8) -- (2.6,0);
\node[above] at (4.0,1.8) {Image};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

If $0<k<1$ the image is a **reduction** — the same shape, but every side is shorter.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (2.8,0) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (2.8,0) -- (2.8,1.8);
\draw (2.8,1.8) -- (0,1.8);
\draw (0,1.8) -- (0,0);
\node[above] at (1.4,1.8) {Original};
\draw (4.0,0) -- (5.4,0) node[midway,below] {$ka$};
\draw (5.4,0) -- (5.4,0.9);
\draw (5.4,0.9) -- (4.0,0.9);
\draw (4.0,0.9) -- (4.0,0);
\node[above] at (4.7,0.9) {Image};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

If $k=1$ the image is **congruent** to the original — the same shape and the same size.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) -- (1.6,0) node[midway,below] {$a$};
\draw (1.6,0) -- (1.6,1.0);
\draw (1.6,1.0) -- (0,1.0);
\draw (0,1.0) -- (0,0);
\node[above] at (0.8,1.0) {Original};
\draw (2.8,0) -- (4.4,0) node[midway,below] {$ka$};
\draw (4.4,0) -- (4.4,1.0);
\draw (4.4,1.0) -- (2.8,1.0);
\draw (2.8,1.0) -- (2.8,0);
\node[above] at (3.6,1.0) {Image};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The original rectangle is $5$ cm wide. Its image is $15$ cm wide. The scale factor from original to image is $3$. Find $x$, the image height, and $y$, the original width of a different figure whose corresponding image side is $21$ cm.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (1.5,1.2);
\path (0.75,0) node[below] {$5\text{ cm}$} (0,0.6) node[left] {$4\text{ cm}$} (0.75,1.2) node[above] {Original};
\draw (3,0) rectangle (6,2.4);
\path (4.5,0) node[below] {$15\text{ cm}$} (6,1.2) node[right] {$x\text{ cm}$} (4.5,2.4) node[above] {Image};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Diagrams are not drawn to scale.

